# t03_led_bright_rgb.ledc -- BRIGHTNESS LINEARITY and the R/G/B fields.  ~71 s
# NOT therapeutic.  Feature family: LED carrier (colour + level).
#
# THE POINT.  Light output tracks "effective drive" = bright x RGB/255, but only
# above drive ~100 (ledc_format.md "bright is linear only above ~40% effective
# drive"):
#       drive  12 -> 0.135 light/drive  (68% of linear)
#       drive  24 -> 0.153              (77%)
#       drive  51 -> 0.183..0.188       (92-95%)
#       drive 102 -> 0.200              (100%)
#       drive 255 -> 0.197..0.202       (100%)
# So brightness is graded by stepping ONE lane through the table while the other
# lane holds a FIXED reference, and amplitudes are compared WITHIN one sensor
# across time.  Never across the two sensors: ch1 and ch2 are not gain-matched
# (they differ by a constant 0.33 ms in timing and by tens of dB in level).
#
# lane A = mask 3  (LED1+LED2, capture ch4) -- the variable under test, 10 Hz
# lane B = mask 12 (LED3+LED4, capture ch3) -- fixed reference, 7 Hz
# Distinct rates so a spectrum cannot confuse the two.
#
#
#
# CAPTURE-INTEGRITY CARRIER (audio ch15, 1900 Hz sine, hard LEFT, vol 40, from
# t=700 ms to the end of the file).  This is measurement scaffolding, not
# content.  A recorder can lose a block of frames mid-capture and still write a
# file of exactly the right length -- the missing TIME is simply absent -- and
# the frame-count check is blind to it.  Five such steps were found in one 665 s
# capture (~1 per 2.2 min), and one of them was written up as a firmware defect
# because BOTH optical sensors agreed on it: the whole recording had stepped.
#   python3 tools/avmeasure/captureintegrity.py rec.wav --carrier 1900 --audio-ch 1
# The tool needs a CONTINUOUS SINE: a session cycling square/saw/noise carriers
# gives ~369 us of phase noise against ~1 us for a sine, which inflates its
# self-calibrating threshold into a rubber stamp.  Hard LEFT so the RIGHT capture
# channel stays free of it for envelope and avsync work.  The marker window
# (0..600 ms) is not covered by this carrier; check it separately with
# --carrier 3000.
# CAPTURE-CHANNEL MAP used throughout sessions/test (see README.md):
#   capture ch1,ch2 = device headphone jack L,R   capture ch3 = photocell on
#   lane B's eye (LED3+LED4, mask 12)             capture ch4 = photocell on
#   lane A's eye (LED1+LED2, mask 3).
# --------------------------------------------------------------- sync marker
0     40 50 100 255 255 255 15
A 0   3000 0 70 0 1
600   -1 50 0 0 0 0 15
A 600 0 0 0 0 1

# ---- capture-integrity carrier: continuous 1900 Hz sine, ch15, hard LEFT
A 700 1900 -100 40 0 15 0 0

# ---- ISR TICK PRIMER.  The flicker gptimer is DELETED whenever no channel is
#      left running and the next activation re-derives its tick from the 1000 Hz
#      FLOOR, so the marker's `freq 0` stop at 600 ms RESETS it -- the 40 Hz
#      marker does NOT pin the tick.  Worse, an update on an already-running
#      channel never calls s_ensure_timer_and_task, so whatever the FIRST
#      post-marker block sets is what every later block gets.
#      This row keeps ONE channel alive at 40 Hz for the whole file, pinning the
#      tick at clamp(40*250,1000,10000) = 10000 Hz = a 100 us grid everywhere
#      below.  mask 16 = LED5, which neither sensor can see (-78 / -74 dBFS, the
#      noise floor), and brightness is 0 regardless, so it cannot contaminate a
#      measurement on any unit.  It raises LEDC_MASK_HIGH_BITS (info) by design.
1000 40 50 0 0 0 0 16 0 0

# ---- lane B: ONE entry, constant for the whole file.  7 Hz, RGB 255,
#      bright 100 (drive 255, dead centre of the linear region).  Every lane-A
#      amplitude below is quoted as a ratio to this, which divides out any slow
#      sensor or supply drift.
1000  7 50 100 255 255 255 12 0 0

# ---- B1  1..11 s : lane A drive 255 (bright 100, RGB 255) -- the 1.00 anchor
1000 10 50 100 255 255 255 3 0 0

# ---- B2  11..21 s : lane A drive 51 (bright 20, RGB 255) -- expect 92-95% of
#      linear, i.e. light ratio 0.184..0.188 of B1, not 0.200
11000 10 50 20 255 255 255 3 0 0

# ---- B3  21..31 s : lane A drive 12.75 (bright 5, RGB 255) -- expect ~68% of
#      linear, light ratio ~0.034 of B1 against a linear prediction of 0.050
21000 10 50 5 255 255 255 3 0 0

# ---- B4  31..41 s : EQUAL-DRIVE CROSS-CHECK, part 1.  bright 24 x RGB 255 =
#      drive 61.2.
31000 10 50 24 255 255 255 3 0 0

# ---- B5  41..51 s : EQUAL-DRIVE CROSS-CHECK, part 2.  bright 100 x RGB 60 =
#      drive 60.  B4 and B5 must land within a few percent of each other: that
#      is what proves the roll-off is the LED's own PWM behaviour and not a
#      measurement-chain non-linearity (ledc_format.md "Validated as a device
#      property rather than a sensor artifact").
41000 10 50 100 60 60 60 3 0 0

# ---- B6  51..61 s : COLOUR FIELDS.  Pure red on lane A, pure green on lane B.
#      lane B leaves its reference only for this block and the next.
51000 10 50 100 255 0 0 3 0 0          # lane A: R only
51000  7 50 100 0 255 0 12 0 0         # lane B: G only

# ---- B7  61..71 s : pure blue on lane A, lane B back to white reference.
61000 10 50 100 0 0 255 3 0 0          # lane A: B only
61000  7 50 100 255 255 255 12 0 0     # lane B: reference restored

# ---- end
71000 -1 50 0 0 0 0 15
71000 -1 50 0 0 0 0 16                     # stop the tick primer too
A 71000 1900 -100 0 0 15 0 0            # stop the integrity carrier
